dogscoff <dogscoff@eudoramail.com> writes:
> On May 4, 3:25 pm, Jaina <j...@magic.ms> wrote:

> > The point is that while bonefiles are a funny feature and kind of work
> > for an open server, they make no sense at all on a local machine where

> > So I wonder why there isn't a simply define statement in the nethack
> > config? Where can I disable them easily?

> Easiest thing would be, as you say, to delete them before starting
> every game. Easiest way to do *that* is to replace your nethack
> startup icon/command for a script that deletes any and all bones files
> present before starting the game up for you.

That depends on the platform.
On many multiuser platforms deleting the bones requires privileges that
are not (and shouldn't be) granted to the startup script.
In that case, it makes more sense to patch the game to disable bones
creation. If no bones are created, there will be no bones to load once the
already-existing ones have been consumed or otherwise deleted. Of course,
bones loading could also be disabled.

One simple way (but not the only one or even the best one) to disable
bones creation could be modifying the can_make_bones function in bones.c
to always unconditionally return false. 

And a similar way to disable bones loading could be making the getbones
function in bones.c to always return 0 instead of doing anything else it
does.

Disclaimer: I haven't tested either of those. They were just my first
thoughts for a patch after a quick look at bones.c.

